Inspirations
------------

No code, artwork or audio was taken from any of these.

Compact Cassette playback (docs/REFERENCE.md)
  Tape speed, shell size, tape lengths and thicknesses, the 120 and 70
  microsecond playback equalisations, and the published behaviour of the
  B-type and C-type noise reduction systems, from the sources listed there.
  The noise reduction is modelled from those descriptions, not from any
  circuit or licensed implementation.

The cassette deck itself
  The deck is generic: no real model is copied, and no maker's name, logo or
  wordmark appears on it. The noise-reduction switch reads "NR B / C". Dolby
  is a trademark of Dolby Laboratories; "Compact Cassette" is a trademark of
  its owner. This project is not affiliated with or endorsed by either, or
  by any maker of cassette decks.

Ultra-Realistic Vinyl Player, by Lucas Van De Weerd
  The project this one is built from (same author, MIT). Its code was copied
  and adapted: the audio engine, the worklet's delay line and read head, the
  library, queue, Winamp EQ, visualizers, desktop, room, lighting, camera,
  post-processing, procedural surfaces, page chrome and the headless check
  harness.
  https://github.com/Gitguy268/ultra-realistic-vinyl-player

henryjeff/portfolio-website
  Its technique and discipline (one continuous 3D world, a slow camera, custom
  shaders, restrained post-processing) guided the approach. The repository
  has no licence, so nothing was taken from it: no code, content, layout or
  palette.
  https://github.com/henryjeff/portfolio-website

Winampify (MIT)
  The library desktop, where artists and albums are folders and tracks are
  files to drag onto the deck, follows its idea. No code was taken.
  https://github.com/remigallego/winampify

awesome-audio-visualization (CC0)
  The Lines (ridgeline), Oscilloscope, Spectrogram and Radial label
  visualizers are ideas found through this list, among them its "Lines"
  experiment and the "Awesome Audio Player" and "CircleLineNERDDISCO" pens.
  They are drawn from scratch; no code was taken from the list's entries.
  https://github.com/willianjusten/awesome-audio-visualization

Classic VU meters
  The 300 ms needle ballistics of the deck's meters follow the traditional
  VU behaviour.

